dom is a FILE* at 0x41e0, opened from /dev/urandom in init()\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() pre-creates 5 cookies via strdup with sizes 0x50, 0x30, 0x18, 0x38, 0x40\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie checks cookie_num <= 31 and message_length <= 256 (both signed comparisons)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie stores message_length as int (4 bytes) in msg_size[] via mov ecx,edx, truncating from 8-byte long long\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads idx via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long, checks idx < cookie_num (unsigned jl)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads into msg[idx] buffer with size msg_size[idx]-1 via read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads idx via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long, checks idx < cookie_num (unsigned jl), then printf(%s, msg[idx])\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie selects a random index via fread(urandom, 1 byte) % cookie_num, frees msg[idx], then moves msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[idx] and decrements cookie_num\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie does NOT null out msg[cookie_num-1] after moving its pointer to msg[idx]\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"alarm(60) is called in init(), setting a 60-second timeout\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"main loop: calls menu(), scanf(%lld) for choice, dispatches to eat/create/edit/read/exit\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F018\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg array (0x4060, 256 bytes) and msg_size array (0x4160, 128 bytes) are contiguous in .bss with no gap between them\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F019\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"msg_size[-1] relative to msg_size base would be at 0x415c, which is still within the msg array (0x4060..0x4160), specifically msg[0x415c-0x4060] = msg[0xfc] = msg[252] bytes offset = element 31.5 — this means negative indexing of msg_size overlaps with msg pointer entries\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F020\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie use unsigned comparison (jl after cmp rdx,rax) for idx < cookie_num, meaning negative values wrapped as unsigned could pass the check if cookie_num is large enough\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F021\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"The GOT is at 0x3f58 with Full RELRO, meaning GOT entries are read-only at runtime\",\"source\":\"ELF\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether ASLR is enabled at runtime\",\"reason\":\"Cannot confirm from ELF alone; requires runtime observation of address randomization\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether seccomp or other sandbox restrictions are in place\",\"reason\":\"No seccomp filter detected in ELF, but runtime restrictions cannot be ruled out without execution environment inspection\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Whether the unsigned idx comparison in edit_cookie/read_cookie allows out-of-bounds access when cookie_num is small (e.g., idx=0 with cookie_num=5 passes, but large unsigned values that wrap could also pass)\",\"reason\":\"The comparison is unsigned jl (idx < cookie_num); if idx is a very large unsigned value and cookie_num is small, it would fail; but the exact behavior depends on runtime cookie_num value and attacker-provided idx\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"Whether eat_cookie's compaction (moving msg[last] to msg[freed_idx] without clearing msg[last]) creates a dangling pointer that could be exploited\",\"reason\":\"S2 needs to analyze the control flow: if cookie_num is decremented, msg[last] is logically out-of-bounds, but the stale pointer value remains in memory; whether this is reachable depends on subsequent operations\"},{\"id\":\"U005\",\"description\":\"Whether the truncation of message_length from long long (8 bytes) to int (4 bytes) in create_cookie creates a type confusion or size mismatch\",\"reason\":\"scanf reads %lld (8 bytes) but msg_size stores only 4 bytes (int); if message_length fits in int this is benign, but S2 should verify edge cases\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 x86-64 PIE, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped, GCC 10.2.0, entry 0x1110\",\"sections\":\"29 sections; .text@0x1110(0x891 AX), .rodata@0x2000(0x266 A), .data@0x4000(0x10 WA), .bss@0x4020(0x1d0 WA), .got@0x3f58(0xa8 WA), .plt@0x1020(0xe0 AX)\",\"symbols\":\"Key symbols: _abort@0x11f5, init@0x1232, create_cookie@0x1399, eat_cookie@0x14e1, edit_cookie@0x1628, read_cookie@0x1751, menu@0x17fd, main@0x187f; Globals: msg@0x4060(256B), msg_size@0x4160(128B), urandom@0x41e0(8B), cookie_num@0x41e8(8B)\",\"disassembly\":\"main: loop(menu->scanf(%lld)->dispatch 1-5); init: setvbuf*3, alarm(60), fopen(/dev/urandom), strdup*5 for initial cookies, cookie_num=5; create_cookie: check cookie_num<=31, scanf(%lld) size<=256, calloc(size), read(0,buf,size), store in msg[msg_num]/msg_size[cookie_num], cookie_num++; eat_cookie: fread(urandom,1,1), idx=byte%cookie_num, printf(msg[idx]), free(msg[idx]), msg[idx]=msg[cookie_num-1], msg_size[idx]=msg_size[cookie_num-1], cookie_num--; edit_cookie: printf range [0-cookie_num-1], scanf(%llu) idx, check idx<cookie_num, read(0,msg[idx],msg_size[idx]-1); read_cookie: scanf(%llu) idx, check idx<cookie_num, printf(%s,msg[idx])\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":3,\"high_severity\":2,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB Signedness Mismatch in edit_cookie and read_cookie (idx read as unsigned but checked with signed comparison)\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"idx is read via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long, but the bounds check uses 'jl' (signed less-than jump) after 'cmp rdx, rax'. When idx is a very large unsigned value (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF = -1 signed), the signed comparison treats it as negative, passing the check (idx < cookie_num). The idx value is then used as an unsigned 64-bit offset to index into msg[] (8-byte stride) and msg_size[] (4-byte stride), causing out-of-bounds access to memory before the arrays.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (pointer array at 0x4060, 32 elements)\",\"msg_size (int array at 0x4160, 32 elements)\",\"stdout (FILE* at 0x4020)\",\"stdin (FILE* at 0x4030)\",\"stderr (FILE* at 0x4040)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(%llu) in edit_cookie and read_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie / read_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker provides idx via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long\",\"idx stored in [rbp-0x10] (8 bytes)\",\"cmp rdx, rax compares idx (rdx) against cookie_num (rax)\",\"jl (signed less-than) passes when idx is a large unsigned value interpreted as negative\",\"idx used in lea rdx, [rax*8+0x0] to compute msg[idx] address (unsigned multiplication)\",\"msg[idx] pointer loaded from OOB memory\",\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) writes attacker data to OOB pointer target\",\"read_cookie: printf(%s, msg[idx]) reads and outputs data from OOB pointer target\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"idx should be checked with unsigned comparison (jb/jae) to ensure idx < cookie_num for unsigned values\",\"actual\":\"idx is checked with jl (signed less-than), allowing large unsigned values (interpreted as negative signed) to pass the check\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Incorrect comparison instruction: jl (signed) instead of jb (unsigned). The format specifier %llu reads unsigned, but the comparison treats the value as signed.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array access and pointer dereference\",\"address\":\"0x1681 (edit_cookie cmp), 0x17aa (read_cookie cmp), 0x16c3/16d2 (edit_cookie msg[idx] access), 0x17bf/17ce (read_cookie msg[idx] access)\",\"expression\":\"msg[idx] where idx is attacker-controlled unsigned value that bypassed signed comparison; edit_cookie: read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1); read_cookie: printf(%s, msg[idx])\",\"target\":\"msg[] array (0x4060) and msg_size[] array (0x4160) accessed out-of-bounds with negative indices; msg[-8] = stdout FILE* (0x4020), msg[-4] = stderr FILE* (0x4040)\",\"size\":\"msg_size[idx]-1 for edit_cookie (when msg_size[idx]=0, size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF via movsxd sign-extension)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"read_cookie with negative idx loads an OOB pointer from msg[] (e.g., stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers) and passes it to printf(%s), leaking memory content at that address. Dynamically verified: idx=-8 caused printf to output binary data from the stdout FILE structure.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"edit_cookie with negative idx loads an OOB pointer from msg[] (e.g., stdout FILE* at 0x4020) and calls read(0, OOB_pointer, msg_size[idx]-1). When msg_size[idx]=0, the size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (sign-extended -1), allowing a massive write to the OOB pointer target. Dynamically verified: idx=-8 caused read() to write attacker data to the stdout FILE structure address.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"read_cookie with negative idx prints data from an OOB pointer via printf(%s), potentially leaking libc/heap addresses stored in global variables like stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"edit_cookie writes attacker-controlled data to arbitrary addresses pointed to by OOB msg[] entries, corrupting memory at those addresses (e.g., FILE structures, other global data).\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"idx (rdx, from scanf %llu) is compared against cookie_num (rax) with 'cmp rdx, rax; jl 0x1692'. jl is a signed comparison, allowing large unsigned idx values (interpreted as negative signed) to pass the check.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Same pattern: 'cmp rdx, rax; jl 0x17bb'. idx from scanf(%llu) checked with signed jl.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16d2\",\"description\":\"After the check passes, idx is used unsigned: 'mov rax, [rbp-0x10]; lea rdx, [rax*4+0x0]' for msg_size[idx] and 'lea rcx, [rax*8+0x0]' for msg[idx]. The unsigned multiplication produces OOB addresses for negative idx values.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16de\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx] is loaded, decremented by 1, and sign-extended via movsxd to 64-bit for read() size. When msg_size[idx]=0, size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1684\",\"description\":\"GDB breakpoint at 0x1684: with input idx=18446744073709551608 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8 signed), rdx=0xfffffffffffffff8, rax=0x5. The jl check passed (-8 < 5 signed). Program proceeded to 'New Message: Done!' without crashing, confirming OOB access succeeded.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17ad\",\"description\":\"GDB breakpoint at 0x17ad: with input idx=18446744073709551608 (-8 signed), rdx=0xfffffffffffffff8, rax=0x5. The jl check passed. printf output binary data '(­û' from the stdout FILE structure, confirming OOB read and info leak.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6\",\"description\":\"GDB at 0x16b6: with idx=-8, msg_size[-8] at 0x4140 = 0x00000000. After sub eax,0x1 and movsxd, the read() size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. msg[-8] at 0x4020 = stdout FILE* = 0x00007ffff7fa75c0. read(0, 0x7ffff7fa75c0, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) was called and succeeded.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1665\",\"description\":\"Format string at 0x21c4 is '%llu' (unsigned long long), confirming idx is read as unsigned. The comparison at 0x1684 uses jl (signed), creating the signedness mismatch.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm whether the OOB write via edit_cookie can corrupt FILE structures (stdout/stdin/stderr) in a way that affects program behavior beyond crash\",\"Verify which specific global variables and pointers are accessible via different negative idx values\",\"Determine if msg_size OOB read can return non-zero values that limit the write size, or if zero values (leading to -1 size) are the common case\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"Use-After-Free via eat_cookie compaction (stale duplicate pointer not cleared)\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie frees msg[random_idx] and compacts by moving msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_idx]. However, msg[cookie_num-1] is NOT cleared after the copy, leaving a duplicate pointer. If a subsequent eat_cookie call's random_idx equals the current cookie_num-1 (the stale slot), it frees the duplicate pointer. Any other in-range slot that received the same pointer during a prior compaction now holds a dangling pointer to freed memory.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (pointer array at 0x4060)\",\"msg_size (int array at 0x4160)\",\"cookie_num (at 0x41e8)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"Repeated eat_cookie invocations (option 1) — index is from /dev/urandom (not directly attacker-controlled), but attacker controls timing and number of calls\",\"entry_function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"eat_cookie call 1: random_idx=A (A != cookie_num-1). free(msg[A]). msg[A] = msg[cookie_num-1]. msg[cookie_num-1] NOT cleared (duplicate pointer). cookie_num--.\",\"Now msg[A] (in-range) and msg[old_cookie_num-1] (out-of-range) both point to same block P.\",\"eat_cookie call 2: random_idx = new_cookie_num-1 = old_cookie_num-1. free(msg[old_cookie_num-1]) = free(P). msg[old_cookie_num-1] = msg[new_cookie_num-1] (compaction). cookie_num--.\",\"Now msg[A] (still in-range if A < new_cookie_num) holds dangling pointer to freed block P.\",\"edit_cookie(msg[A]) or read_cookie(msg[A]) accesses freed memory → UAF.\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After moving msg[cookie_num-1] to msg[random_idx], msg[cookie_num-1] should be set to NULL to prevent duplicate/stale pointer\",\"actual\":\"msg[cookie_num-1] is not cleared after the copy, leaving a duplicate pointer that can be freed again by a subsequent eat_cookie call\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing NULL assignment to msg[cookie_num-1] after compaction copy\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Free of duplicate pointer followed by dereference via in-range slot\",\"address\":\"0x158e (free in eat_cookie), 0x15c6 (msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1] copy without clearing source)\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[random_idx]); msg[random_idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]; /* msg[cookie_num-1] not cleared */\",\"target\":\"msg[cookie_num-1] retains duplicate pointer; subsequent free of this slot creates dangling pointer in msg[A]\",\"size\":\"8 bytes (pointer)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"If UAF occurs, read_cookie on the dangling pointer slot reads from freed heap memory, potentially leaking freed heap content or metadata.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"If UAF occurs, edit_cookie on the dangling pointer slot writes to freed heap memory via read(0, dangling_ptr, msg_size[idx]-1), corrupting freed heap data.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"Writing to freed heap memory corrupts heap metadata and potentially overlapping allocations.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"Dereferencing a dangling pointer after the freed block is reused or unmapped may cause SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15b0-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"Compaction code: 'mov rax, [rcx+rax*1]' loads msg[cookie_num-1], then 'mov QWORD PTR [rcx+rdx*1], rax' stores it to msg[random_idx]. No instruction clears msg[cookie_num-1] (no 'mov QWORD PTR [...], 0' for the source slot).\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15ff-0x160a\",\"description\":\"cookie_num is decremented ('sub rax,0x1; mov [rip+...],rax') without clearing msg[old_cookie_num-1]. The stale pointer remains in the now-out-of-range slot.\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"GDB trace of 4 successive eat_cookie calls: after call 1 (random_idx=0, cookie_num 5→4), msg[4]=0x555555559580 (not cleared, duplicate of msg[0]=0x555555559580). After call 2 (random_idx=0, cookie_num 4→3), msg[4] still = 0x555555559580 (now freed block). Stale pointer persists across calls.\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15c6\",\"description\":\"If call 1 has random_idx=A (A != cookie_num-1), msg[A] and msg[cookie_num-1] both point to same block. If call 2 has random_idx=cookie_num-1, it frees that block. msg[A] (in-range) becomes dangling. This requires random_idx=cookie_num-1 in call 2, probability 1/cookie_num per call. Attacker can retry eat_cookie to achieve this condition.\"}],\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"verification_required\":[\"Verify the exact probability and feasibility of achieving the UAF condition with repeated eat_cookie calls\",\"Confirm whether the freed block from the UAF scenario is accessible via edit_cookie/read_cookie before reallocation\",\"Determine if heap allocator (glibc 2.39 tcache/fastbin) behavior affects the exploitability of the dangling pointer\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"NULL pointer dereference in create_cookie with negative message_length\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"message_length is read via scanf(%lld) as signed long long. The check 'cmp rax, 0x100; jle' uses signed comparison, so negative values (e.g., -1) pass the check (-1 <= 0x100). calloc() receives the negative value as an unsigned size (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), which fails and returns NULL. The code then dereferences the NULL pointer when accessing buf[read_return] to check for newline.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"create_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"calloc buffer (stack local [rbp-0x10])\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"message_length via scanf(%lld) in create_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"create_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker provides message_length = -1 (or any negative value)\",\"scanf(%lld) stores -1 as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF in [rbp-0x18]\",\"cmp rax, 0x100; jle passes (-1 <= 0x100 signed)\",\"calloc(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 1) returns NULL\",\"read(0, NULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) returns -1 or 0\",\"buf[read_return] dereferences NULL + read_return → SIGSEGV\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"message_length should be checked for non-negative values, or comparison should be unsigned (jbe instead of jle)\",\"actual\":\"message_length checked with jle (signed), allowing negative values to pass\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check for message_length (should be >= 0 or >= 1)\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"NULL pointer dereference\",\"address\":\"0x1458\",\"expression\":\"movzx eax, [rax] where rax = NULL_buf + read_return\",\"target\":\"NULL pointer (calloc return for negative size)\",\"size\":\"1 byte read\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Negative message_length causes calloc to return NULL, and subsequent buf[read_return] access dereferences NULL, causing SIGSEGV. Dynamically verified: message_length=-1 caused SIGSEGV at 0x1458.\"},{\"type\":\"DOS\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The crash terminates the process, denying service to legitimate users.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x13f6-0x13fc\",\"description\":\"Size check: 'cmp rax, 0x100; jle 0x140a'. jle is signed comparison, so negative message_length values pass the check.\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1413-0x1416\",\"description\":\"calloc called with rdi=message_length (negative value as unsigned). For -1, rdi=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, calloc fails and returns NULL.\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1455-0x1458\",\"description\":\"'add rax, rdx; movzx eax, [rax]' where rax=NULL (calloc return) and rdx=read_return. Dereferences NULL+read_return.\"},{\"id\":\"E016\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1458\",\"description\":\"GDB: with message_length=-1, rax=0xffffffffffffffff at 0x140a. calloc returned NULL (rdi=0x0 at 0x1416). SIGSEGV at 0x1458 when accessing buf[read_return].\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm whether message_length=0 also causes issues (calloc(0) may return non-NULL but read(0,buf,0) returns 0, then buf[0] is accessed on a 0-size allocation)\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"Menu choice is read via scanf(%lld) and compared with exact values 1-5 using cmp/jne. No vulnerability in the dispatch logic.\",\"reason\":\"Choice values are directly compared against constants; no array indexing or memory operation depends on the choice value.\"},{\"function\":\"init\",\"description\":\"Initializes 5 cookies via strdup and sets cookie_num=5. Opens /dev/urandom. All operations are safe.\",\"reason\":\"No attacker-controlled input reaches init(). strdup returns valid heap pointers. urandom NULL check is present.\"},{\"function\":\"menu\",\"description\":\"Prints menu options via puts(). No input processing.\",\"reason\":\"No attacker-controlled data is processed.\"},{\"function\":\"_abort\",\"description\":\"Prints error message and exits. No input processing.\",\"reason\":\"No attacker-controlled data is processed.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie UAF (BUG-002) can be reliably triggered by repeated eat_cookie calls to achieve the required random_idx sequence\",\"reason\":\"The random index comes from /dev/urandom and is not attacker-controlled. The probability depends on cookie_num and the specific sequence needed.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Statistical analysis of trigger probability across multiple eat_cookie calls, or runtime testing with repeated calls to observe if the UAF condition is achievable in practice\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the OOB write via edit_cookie (BUG-001) with idx=-8 writing to stdout FILE structure causes observable corruption beyond a crash\",\"reason\":\"The write target is the stdout FILE* structure. The exact impact depends on which fields are overwritten and whether the program continues to use stdout afterward.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic verification of which FILE structure fields are corrupted and whether the corruption affects subsequent I/O operations\"},{\"description\":\"Whether negative idx values other than -8 access useful targets (e.g., idx=-4 accesses stderr, idx=-1 accesses memory just before msg)\",\"reason\":\"Different negative indices access different global variables. A systematic mapping of accessible targets would clarify the full attack surface.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Enumerate all accessible OOB targets for negative idx values from -1 to -8 (and beyond) and assess each for security impact\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":3,\"verified\":3,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":0,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch: idx read via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long, but bounds check uses jl (signed less-than). Large unsigned values (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8 signed) pass the check as negative signed values, then are used as unsigned 64-bit offsets to index into msg[] (8-byte stride), causing OOB access to memory before the array.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with idx=-8 bypasses the signed bounds check (jl: -8 < 5 = true), then loads msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* = _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc, and passes it to printf(%s), leaking FILE structure content.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in read_cookie\",\"scanf(%llu) reads idx as unsigned (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8)\",\"cmp rdx,rax; jl passes because -8 < 5 (signed comparison)\",\"idx used unsigned: lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0] computes msg[-8] address = 0x4060 + (-8)*8 = 0x4020\",\"msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* = _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc\",\"printf(%s, stdout_FILE*) outputs FILE struct bytes until null terminator\",\"INFO_LEAK: stdout FILE struct content (including _flags=0xfbad2887) leaked to attacker\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"OOB read target selection via negative idx; leaked data is stdout FILE structure content\",\"how\":\"Attacker provides idx=-8 (as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 via %llu) to read_cookie. The signed jl check passes (-8 < 5). msg[-8] resolves to *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* pointer. printf(%s, stdout_FILE*) outputs FILE struct bytes.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"idx must be a large unsigned value that is negative when interpreted as signed (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8). This passes the jl (signed less-than) check against cookie_num (default 5).\",\"necessity\":\"Required - without a negative-signed idx, the bounds check correctly rejects OOB values.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"cookie_num must be > 0 (default 5 after init). The jl check requires idx < cookie_num (signed). Any negative idx passes since all negative values < any positive cookie_num.\",\"necessity\":\"Required - cookie_num must be positive for the signed comparison to pass with negative idx.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] must point to readable memory. For idx=-8, msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* which points to _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc (always mapped, always readable).\",\"necessity\":\"Required - the loaded pointer must be valid and point to readable memory for printf to output content.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(0x4060 + (-8)*8) = *(0x4020)\",\"alias\":\"stdout FILE* global variable at 0x4020\",\"implication\":\"idx=-8 in read_cookie/edit_cookie accesses the stdout FILE* pointer, which points to _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc. This is a FIXED target determined by the binary's global layout, not an attacker-controlled address.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-4] = *(0x4060 + (-4)*8) = *(0x4040)\",\"alias\":\"stderr FILE* global variable at 0x4040\",\"implication\":\"idx=-4 accesses stderr FILE* → _IO_2_1_stderr_ in libc. Different negative idx values select different FIXED global targets.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-6] = *(0x4060 + (-6)*8) = *(0x4030)\",\"alias\":\"stdin FILE* global variable at 0x4030\",\"implication\":\"idx=-6 accesses stdin FILE* → _IO_2_1_stdin_ in libc.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie with idx=-8 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) and observe output for FILE structure magic bytes (0xfbad2887).\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86-64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled (ASLR on, but leak works regardless)\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"stdout output contains bytes 0x87 0x28 0xad 0xfb (little-endian 0xfbad2887 = FILE _flags magic)\",\"printf(%s) outputs content from _IO_2_1_stdout_ FILE structure in libc\",\"return_code = 0 (program exits normally after leak)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes the binary base address, but the OOB access targets global variables (stdout/stdin/stderr) at fixed offsets from the binary base. The leak works regardless of PIE because the negative idx is relative to msg[] which is at a fixed offset from the binary base. The leaked content (FILE struct in libc) is also at a fixed offset from libc base.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT entries read-only, preventing GOT overwrite. This does not affect the INFO_LEAK primitive, which reads from FILE structures, not GOT.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents execution of shellcode. Does not affect INFO_LEAK primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Stack canary protects return addresses. Does not affect INFO_LEAK primitive, which does not overflow the stack.\"},{\"name\":\"ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"ASLR randomizes libc base, but the leak outputs FILE struct content which includes libc addresses, potentially defeating ASLR for subsequent exploitation (S4 concern).\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x17bb - idx (rdx, from scanf %llu) compared against cookie_num (rax) using signed jl. Large unsigned idx values (interpreted as negative signed) pass the check.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bf-0x17ce\",\"description\":\"lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0] computes msg[idx] address using unsigned multiplication. For idx=-8 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8), this computes 0x4060 + (-8)*8 = 0x4020 = stdout FILE* global.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"GDB breakpoint at printf call: rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 = _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc. printf(%s, stdout_FILE*) called, outputting FILE struct bytes.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC execution: stdout output contains bytes 87 28 ad fb (little-endian 0xfbad2887 = FILE _flags magic). This is non-attacker-provided data from the stdout FILE structure, confirming INFO_LEAK.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"0xfbad2887 (FILE _flags field leaked via printf %s)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"read_cookie(idx=-8) bypasses signed bounds check (jl: -8 < 5 = true). msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* = 0x7ffff7fa75c0 (_IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc). printf(%s, 0x7ffff7fa75c0) outputs FILE struct content. Output contains 0x87 0x28 0xad 0xfb = little-endian 0xfbad2887 (FILE _flags magic). Program exits normally (rc=0). Leak confirmed: attacker receives FILE structure content not provided by attacker.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK is DIRECT: the OOB read directly loads a pointer from a fixed global (stdout FILE*) and passes it to printf. No intermediate object reuse or metadata manipulation needed. The leak is limited by printf(%s) stopping at null bytes - only 3-4 bytes of the _flags field are leaked before the first null. However, the leaked value (0xfbad2887) confirms the read targets the stdout FILE structure in libc, which combined with known libc layout can reveal the libc base address.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch: idx read via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long, but bounds check uses jl (signed less-than). Large unsigned values (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8 signed) pass the check, then idx is used as unsigned offset to index into msg[] and msg_size[], causing OOB write via read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1).\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie with idx=-8 bypasses the signed bounds check, loads msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* as the write destination, and calls read(0, stdout_FILE*, msg_size[-8]-1). When msg_size[-8]=0 (uninitialized), size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF and read() fails. When msg[28] is filled via create_cookie, msg_size[-8] = low32(msg[28]) = non-zero, and read() succeeds, writing attacker data to the stdout FILE structure.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in edit_cookie\",\"scanf(%llu) reads idx as unsigned (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8)\",\"cmp rdx,rax; jl passes because -8 < 5 (signed comparison)\",\"idx used unsigned: lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0] computes msg[-8] address = 0x4060 + (-8)*8 = 0x4020\",\"msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* = _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc\",\"msg_size[-8] at 0x4140 = low 4 bytes of msg[28] (aliasing)\",\"read(0, stdout_FILE*, msg_size[-8]-1) writes attacker data to stdout FILE struct\",\"RELATIVE_WRITE: attacker-controlled data written to FIXED target (stdout FILE struct)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Write value (attacker-controlled via stdin) and write size (controlled via msg_size[-8] alias with msg[28]). Write target is FIXED (stdout FILE* global at 0x4020), NOT attacker-controlled.\",\"how\":\"Attacker provides idx=-8 to select stdout FILE* as write target. Attacker provides data via stdin which read() writes to the FILE struct. Attacker creates 24 cookies to fill msg[28], making msg_size[-8] non-zero (valid size for read()).\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"idx must be a large unsigned value that is negative when interpreted as signed (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8). This passes the jl (signed less-than) check against cookie_num.\",\"necessity\":\"Required - without a negative-signed idx, the bounds check correctly rejects OOB values.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"cookie_num must be > 0 (default 5 after init). The jl check requires idx < cookie_num (signed). Any negative idx passes.\",\"necessity\":\"Required - cookie_num must be positive.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] must point to writable memory. For idx=-8, msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* which points to _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc (writable .data segment of libc).\",\"necessity\":\"Required - the loaded pointer must point to writable memory for read() to succeed.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx] must be non-zero for read() to succeed. When msg_size[-8]=0, size becomes 0-1=0xFFFFFFFF → sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF → read() returns -1 (fails). To get non-zero msg_size[-8], attacker must create >= 24 cookies (filling msg[28] at 0x4140, whose low 4 bytes alias msg_size[-8]).\",\"necessity\":\"Required - without a valid (non-huge) size, read() fails and no write occurs. Creating 24 cookies fills msg[28] with a heap pointer, making msg_size[-8] = low32(heap_ptr) = non-zero, giving a valid (large) size for read().\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-05\",\"description\":\"The write target (stdout FILE struct) must be writable. _IO_2_1_stdout_ is in libc's .data segment which is writable.\",\"necessity\":\"Required - read() will fail with EFAULT if the target is not writable.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-8] at 0x4160 + (-8)*4 = 0x4140\",\"alias\":\"low 4 bytes of msg[28] at 0x4060 + 28*8 = 0x4140\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-8] and msg[28] overlap at address 0x4140. When msg[28] is filled with a heap pointer (e.g., 0x00005555555598b0) via create_cookie, msg_size[-8] = 0x555598b0 (low 32 bits). This gives a valid (large) size for read(): 0x555598b0 - 1 = 0x555598af. Without this alias, msg_size[-8]=0 → size=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF → read() fails.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-4] at 0x4160 + (-4)*4 = 0x4150\",\"alias\":\"low 4 bytes of msg[30] at 0x4060 + 30*8 = 0x4150\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-4] aliases msg[30]. Creating 26 cookies fills msg[30], making msg_size[-4] non-zero for writes targeting stderr FILE* (idx=-4).\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-2] at 0x4160 + (-2)*4 = 0x4158\",\"alias\":\"low 4 bytes of msg[31] at 0x4060 + 31*8 = 0x4158\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-2] aliases msg[31]. Creating 27 cookies fills msg[31], making msg_size[-2] non-zero.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. Create 24 cookies to fill msg[28] (making msg_size[-8] non-zero). 2. Call edit_cookie with idx=-8. 3. Provide data via stdin. 4. Observe crash/corruption confirming write to stdout FILE struct.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86-64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"edit_cookie prints 'Done!' confirming read() completed successfully\",\"Program crashes (SIGSEGV/SIGKILL) due to corrupted stdout FILE struct\",\"GDB: stdout FILE struct _flags changes from 0xfbad2887 to corrupted value after write\",\"GDB: rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (stdout FILE struct), rdx=0x555598af (valid size) at read() call\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes binary base, but the OOB write targets global variables (stdout FILE*) at fixed offsets from binary base. The write works regardless of PIE. The write destination (stdout FILE struct in libc) is at a fixed offset from libc base.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO prevents GOT overwrite, but this primitive writes to FILE structures, not GOT. RELRO does not block this primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents code execution on stack/heap. Does not directly affect the write primitive, but limits what the corrupted FILE struct can be used for (S4 concern).\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Stack canary does not protect global variables or FILE structures. Does not affect this primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"ASLR randomizes libc base, but the write target (stdout FILE*) is loaded from a fixed global variable. The actual write destination in libc is randomized, but the global pointer resolves correctly at runtime.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x1692 - idx (rdx, from scanf %llu) compared against cookie_num (rax) using signed jl. Large unsigned idx values pass as negative signed.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16d2\",\"description\":\"lea rdx,[rax*4+0x0] for msg_size[idx] and lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0] for msg[idx]. Unsigned multiplication produces OOB addresses for negative idx.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16de\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx] loaded, sub eax,0x1, movsxd rdx,eax (sign-extend to 64-bit). When msg_size[idx]=0, size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. read(0, msg[idx], size) called.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB at read() call: edi=0 (stdin fd), rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (stdout FILE struct = _IO_2_1_stdout_), rdx=0xffffffffffffffff (size, when msg_size[-8]=0). read() returns -1 (fails due to huge size).\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB after creating 24 cookies: rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (stdout FILE struct), rdx=0x555598af (valid size = low32(msg[28])-1). read() succeeds. stdout FILE struct _flags changed from 0xfbad2887 to 0xfbad2084, confirming write.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"PoC execution: edit_cookie(idx=-8) with 24 cookies created. Program printed 'Done!' (read completed), then crashed with SIGKILL (rc=-9) due to corrupted stdout FILE struct causing massive output (460MB). Confirms write to stdout FILE struct succeeded.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"0x58585858 (XXXX from stdin)\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":-9,\"details\":\"edit_cookie(idx=-8) with 24 cookies created: msg[-8]=*(0x4020)=stdout FILE*=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (_IO_2_1_stdout_). msg_size[-8]=low32(msg[28])=0x555598b0 (non-zero, valid size). read(0, 0x7ffff7fa75c0, 0x555598af) called and succeeded. GDB confirmed stdout FILE struct _flags changed from 0xfbad2887 to 0xfbad2084 after write. PoC: program printed 'Done!' then crashed (SIGKILL, rc=-9) due to corrupted stdout causing 460MB output. Write target is FIXED (stdout FILE* global), not attacker-controlled → RELATIVE_WRITE, not ARB_WRITE.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE (not ARB_WRITE) because: (1) Write target is a FIXED global variable (stdout FILE* at 0x4020), determined by the binary's memory layout. Attacker selects which fixed target via idx (-8=stdout, -4=stderr, -6=stdin) but cannot specify arbitrary addresses. (2) Write value is attacker-controlled (from stdin). (3) Write size is partially controlled via msg_size[-8] alias with msg[28] (creating cookies sets the size). The write corrupts the stdout FILE structure, which is a DIRECT primitive (no intermediate object reuse needed). The alias between msg_size[-8] and msg[28] is a critical precondition: without creating 24 cookies, msg_size[-8]=0 → size=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF → read() fails.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_READ\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same signedness mismatch as PRIM-001/PRIM-002. read_cookie with negative idx loads an OOB pointer from msg[] and passes it to printf(%s), reading and outputting memory content at that fixed OOB location.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with idx=-8 reads from stdout FILE struct (same as INFO_LEAK). With idx=-4, it reads from stderr FILE struct. The read target is FIXED (determined by global variable layout), not attacker-controlled. This is classified as RELATIVE_READ because the read is limited to fixed OOB locations (stdout/stdin/stderr FILE structs), not arbitrary addresses.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in read_cookie\",\"scanf(%llu) reads idx as unsigned (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8)\",\"cmp rdx,rax; jl passes because -8 < 5 (signed)\",\"idx used unsigned: lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0] computes msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE*\",\"printf(%s, stdout_FILE*) reads and outputs FILE struct content\",\"RELATIVE_READ: reads from FIXED OOB location (stdout FILE struct)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Read target selection via negative idx (fixed set: stdout/stdin/stderr FILE structs). Read content is output to attacker via printf.\",\"how\":\"Attacker provides idx=-8 (stdout), idx=-6 (stdin), or idx=-4 (stderr) to select which fixed FILE structure to read from.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"idx must be a large unsigned value that is negative when interpreted as signed (e.g., -8, -6, -4).\",\"necessity\":\"Required - to bypass the signed jl bounds check.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"cookie_num must be > 0 (default 5).\",\"necessity\":\"Required - for the signed comparison to pass with negative idx.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] mus